(function(){window.PhysicaRankPyqNeet_zoology_digestionandabsorption=[{id:"neet-2024-q161",pn:161,y:"NEET 2024 \xB7 5 May",tp:"Digestion and Absorption",st:"Digestive enzymes and bonds",q:`Match List I with List II :
A. Lipase \u2014 I. Peptide bond
B. Nuclease \u2014 II. Ester bond
C. Protease \u2014 III. Glycosidic bond
D. Amylase \u2014 IV. Phosphodiester bond`,o:["A-II, B-IV, C-I, D-III","A-IV, B-I, C-III, D-II","A-IV, B-II, C-III, D-I","A-III, B-II, C-I, D-IV"],c:0,s:`Option (1) is correct.
A. Lipase digests the ester bonds of lipids.
B. Nuclease digests the phosphodiester bonds of nucleic acids.
C. Protease digests the peptide bonds of proteins.
D. Amylase breaks the glycosidic bonds of carbohydrates.
It turns starch into smaller molecules and finally maltose, which maltase splits into two glucose molecules.`},{id:"neet-2023-q156",pn:156,y:"NEET 2023 \xB7 7 May",tp:"Digestion and Absorption",st:"Gastric gland cells",q:`Match List I with List II.
List I (Cells): A. Peptic cells; B. Goblet cells; C. Oxyntic cells; D. Hepatic cells
List II (Secretion): I. Mucus; II. Bile Juice; III. Proenzyme pepsinogen; IV. HCl and intrinsic factor for absorption of vitamin B12.
Choose the correct answer from the options given below :`,o:["A-II, B-IV, C-I, D-III","A-IV, B-III, C-II, D-I","A-II, B-I, C-III, D-IV","A-III, B-I, C-IV, D-II"],c:3,s:`Gastric glands have three main cell types.
Mucus neck cells (goblet cells) secrete mucus.
Peptic (chief) cells secrete the proenzyme pepsinogen.
Oxyntic (parietal) cells secrete HCl and intrinsic factor, needed for vitamin B12 absorption.
Hepatic cells make bile juice.`},{id:"neet-2023-q172",pn:172,y:"NEET 2023 \xB7 7 May",tp:"Digestion and Absorption",st:"Large intestine and sphincters",q:"Once the undigested and unabsorbed substances enter the caecum, their backflow is prevented by",o:["Pyloric sphincter","Sphincter of Oddi","Ileo-caecal valve","Gastro-oesophageal sphincter"],c:2,s:`Undigested food passes into the caecum through the ileo-caecal valve, which stops it flowing back.
Gastro-oesophageal sphincter guards the oesophagus-stomach opening.
Pyloric sphincter guards the stomach-duodenum opening.
Sphincter of Oddi guards the common hepato-pancreatic duct opening.`},{id:"neet-2022r-q174",pn:174,y:"Re-NEET 2022 \xB7 4 Sep",tp:"Digestion and Absorption",st:"Structure of tooth",q:"Role of enamel is to :",o:["Give basic shape to the teeth","Connect crown of tooth with its root","Masticate the food","Form bolus"],c:2,s:`Enamel covers the outer side of the crown of the tooth.
It helps in chewing (mastication) of food.`},{id:"neet-2022-q175",pn:175,y:"NEET 2022 \xB7 17 Jul",tp:"Digestion and Absorption",st:"Absorption of fats",q:`Given below are two statements :
Statement I : Fatty acids and glycerols cannot be absorbed into the blood.
Statement II : Specialized lymphatic capillaries called lacteals carry chylomicrons into lymphatic vessels and ultimately into the blood.
In the light of the above statements, choose the most appropriate answer from the options given below:`,o:["Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are correct"],c:3,s:`Fatty acids and glycerol are insoluble in water, so they cannot be absorbed directly into the blood.
They first form micelles that move into the intestinal mucosa.
There they are rebuilt into protein-coated fat globules called chylomicrons.
Chylomicrons enter the lacteals (lymph vessels) in the villi, which finally release them into the blood.
So both statements are correct.`},{id:"neet-2022r-q175",pn:175,y:"Re-NEET 2022 \xB7 4 Sep",tp:"Digestion and Absorption",st:"Digestive enzymes and substrates",q:"Choose the incorrect enzymatic reaction.",o:["Dipeptides \u2192(Dipeptidases) Amino acids","Maltose \u2192(Maltase) Glucose + Galactose","Sucrose \u2192(Sucrase) Glucose + Fructose","Lactose \u2192(Lactase) Glucose + Galactose"],c:1,s:`Maltase splits maltose into two glucose molecules, not glucose and galactose.
Dipeptidase splits dipeptides into amino acids.
Sucrase splits sucrose into glucose and fructose.
Lactase splits lactose into glucose and galactose.`},{id:"neet-2022-q183",pn:183,y:"NEET 2022 \xB7 17 Jul",tp:"Digestion and Absorption",st:"Salivary digestion",q:"Which of the following functions in not performed by secretions from salivary glands?",o:["Digestion of complex carbohydrates","Lubrication of oral cavity","Digestion of disaccharides","Control bacterial population in mouth"],c:2,s:`Saliva digests starch (a complex carbohydrate), so (1) is performed.
Its mucus lubricates the mouth, so (2) is performed.
Its lysozyme checks bacteria, so (4) is performed.
Disaccharides are digested in the small intestine, not by saliva, so (3) is the answer.`},{id:"neet-2021-q153",pn:153,y:"NEET 2021 \xB7 12 Sep",tp:"Digestion and Absorption",st:"Digestive juices",q:"Succus entericus is referred to as:",o:["Chyme","Pancreatic juice","Intestinal juice","Gastric juice"],c:2,s:`Succus entericus is the intestinal juice secreted by the small intestine.
Chyme is the acidic food in the stomach, pancreatic juice comes from the pancreas and gastric juice from the stomach.`},{id:"neet-2021-q175",pn:175,y:"NEET 2021 \xB7 12 Sep",tp:"Digestion and Absorption",st:"Sphincter of Oddi",q:"Sphincter of oddi is present at:",o:["Junction of jejunum and duodenum","Ileo-caecal junction","Junction of hepato-pancreatic duct and duodenum","Gastro-oesophageal junction"],c:2,s:`The bile duct and pancreatic duct join as the hepato-pancreatic duct, which opens into the duodenum.
The sphincter of Oddi guards this opening.`}]})();
